\\ D546_K3_family.gp -- an explicit one-parameter family of elliptic K3 surfaces with Mordell-Weil rank 17 over Q(T),
\\ parametrised by the rational points of an elliptic curve of rank 1.                              (PARI/GP, 2026-09-25)
\\
\\ THE FAMILY.  C : y^2 + x y + y = x^3 - 137 x + 380    (conductor 546;  C(Q) = Z G + C[2],  G = (-6, 34)).
\\   For a point P = (X, Y) of C, the surface X_P over the T-line is
\\         y^2 - (L x + B) y = x (x + D)(x + E),        B = p q (L + p + q) - p E - q D,
\\   i.e. (a1, a2, a3, a4, a6) = (-L, D + E, -B, D E, 0), where L, p, q (degree 2 in T) and D, E (degree 4 in T) are
\\   the polynomials NF below.  Their coefficients are polynomials in X and Y with integer coefficients, so the
\\   model is defined on all of C except the origin O.
\\   17 sections: P1 = (0, 0), P2 = (-D, 0), P3 = (-p q, q (p q - D)), and 14 sections on lines y - y0 = u (x - x0)
\\   through P1 (13 lines) and P2 (1 line).  SLOPES lists them as [k, num, den]: the line through P_k with slope
\\   u(T) = num/den, num in Z[T, X, Y], den in Z[X].  The line meets the curve again where t = x - x0 solves
\\   t^2 - A t - (M u - K) = 0; its discriminant is a square, so both points are sections (line_section below).
\\
\\ THEOREM.  Let K = Q(C) be the function field of C, and X_K the surface over K(T) given by the same formulas.
\\   (1) X_K is an elliptic K3 surface with 24 fibres of type I1: deg a_i <= 2i, and Delta(T) has degree 24 and is
\\       squarefree.  The 14 line discriminants are squares in K[T], so the 17 sections are defined over K(T).
\\   (2) The 17 sections generate the Mordell-Weil group of X_K over Kbar(T): a lattice of rank 17, determinant 1092,
\\       discriminant group Z/1092, without vectors of norm 2.
\\   (3) For every P in C(Q) outside a finite set, X_P is a K3 surface over Q with 24 I1 fibres, and the 17
\\       specialised sections generate its Mordell-Weil group over Q(T), with the same lattice.
\\   (4) The family is not isotrivial.  C(Q) is infinite, so the family contains infinitely many K3 surfaces that are
\\       pairwise non-isomorphic over Qbar.
\\ PROOF.  verify_generic() checks by exact arithmetic in K (a few seconds):
\\   - (1): every section satisfies the equation identically in K[T]; Delta_K has degree 24;
\\   - Delta(P0) = Delta_K(P0) is squarefree of degree 24 at the point P0 = 2G, so disc_T Delta_K != 0;
\\   - the Gram matrix of the 17 sections at P0.  Over the local ring of C at P0 the model is smooth and proper, so
\\     intersection numbers of sections are the same on the generic and on the special fibre: this is the Gram
\\     matrix over K;
\\   - the branch polynomials disc_T(c4^3 - z Delta) at P0 and 3G differ.  They are invariants of the elliptic
\\     surface, so B_K is not constant; with Sterk's theorem (finitely many elliptic fibrations up to automorphisms
\\     on a K3 surface) this gives (4).
\\   For (2): the family is not isotrivial, so the geometric Picard number of X_K is at most 19 and the Mordell-Weil
\\   rank at most 17.  The Mordell-Weil lattice is even and integral (all fibres irreducible: <P,P> = 4 + 2 P.O) and
\\   contains the lattice W of the 17 sections with finite index; W has no even overlattice, so it is everything.
\\   For (3) no further computation is needed; it follows from (1) and (2) by specialisation.  Let Z be the set of
\\   points consisting of O, the poles of the section coordinates (listed by verify_generic()) and the zeros of
\\   disc_T Delta_K.  Z is finite, because disc_T Delta_K is a nonzero element of K by (1).  For P in C(Q) outside Z:
\\     - all coefficients are regular at P and Delta(P) = Delta_K(P) is squarefree of degree 24, so X_P is a K3
\\       surface with 24 I1 fibres;
\\     - the model is smooth and proper over the local ring of C at P, so the specialised sections have the Gram
\\       matrix of (2);
\\     - the Mordell-Weil rank over Q(T) is at most 17.  If X_P has Picard number 19 over Qbar, this is the
\\       Shioda-Tate formula.  If it has Picard number 20, not all of NS is defined over Q, since its field of
\\       definition contains the CM field (M. Schuett, Fields of definition of singular K3 surfaces, 2007).  So
\\       Pic(X_P) has rank at most 19 over Q.  This bound is quoted from the literature, not computed;
\\     - the overlattice argument of (2) then gives MW(X_P/Q(T)) = W.
\\   Z is not listed explicitly, since disc_T Delta_K is too large to expand.  verify(P) certifies any single P
\\   directly: it recomputes Delta and the Gram matrix at P, and uses the same rank bound.
\\
\\ FURTHER FACTS (numerical evidence, not proven here).
\\   - P -> P + T and P -> -G - P + T (T in C[2]) give isomorphic surfaces (equal point counts), so P -> X_P is 8 : 1.
\\     The points mG, m = 2, 3, 4, ..., give pairwise non-isomorphic surfaces over Q (distinct point counts, m <= 10).
\\   - C should be the Atkin-Lehner quotient X_0^546(1)/w_546 of the Shimura curve of discriminant 546: the
\\     transcendental lattice has rank 3 and its even Clifford algebra has discriminant 546.
\\   - Degenerate points among mG + T: m = -2, -1, 0, 1.  O is the pole of the model; at the others the surface has
\\     reducible fibres (CM points; see fibres()).  Every other point mG + T with |m| <= 8 passes verify().
\\   The frame of the T-line is minimised over K.  All coefficients are polynomial on C - O, and <L,L> = L1^2 - 4 L0 L2
\\   (L = L2 T^2 + L1 T + L0) has pole order 30 at O (42 in the first version).  Its zeros are the 26 points where L
\\   is a square, and double zeros at the base point 6G + T1 (where L, p, q vanish) and at the degenerate point T3.
\\   Pole orders at O: L, p, q <= 18, D, E <= 35;  the slopes have no denominators.
\\
\\ USAGE.  gp -q --default parisizemax=1G D546_K3_family.gp       (read it first, in a fresh session)
\\   P = pt(6);                    \\ 6G;   pt(m, t) = mG + T_t,  T_t = TORS[t+1]  (TORS[1] = O)
\\   M = surface(P);               \\ [[L,p,q,D,E,B], [a1,a2,a3,a4,a6], 17 sections [x(T), y(T)]] over Q(T)
\\   verify(P)                     \\ certificate for X_P, and S(p) = #X_P(F_p) - (p+1)^2 at a few primes
\\   verify_generic()              \\ the exact computations over K behind (1)-(4); (3) then follows as in PROOF
\\   MK = generic();               \\ the surface over K: coefficients in Q(X)[Y]/(Ceq)
\\   fibres(surface(pt(-1))[2])    \\ singular fibres at a degenerate point

['T, 'Y, 'X];
if(variables('T + 'Y + 'X) != ['T, 'Y, 'X], error("read D546_K3_family.gp in a fresh gp session (variable priority T > Y > X)"));
CURVE = ellinit([1, 0, 1, -137, 380]);
Ceq = 'Y^2 + ('X + 1)*'Y - ('X^3 - 137*'X + 380);
G = [-6, 34];
TORS = [[0], [-13, 6], [3, -2], [39/4, -43/8]];
pt(m, t = 0) = elladd(CURVE, ellmul(CURVE, G, m), TORS[t + 1]);
DET = 1092;
BASEPTS = [[2, 0], [3, 0]];       \\ the points P0, P1 used by verify_generic()

